<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Fun Outdoors!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Love to Run!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Summarize short stories using drawings or pictures. Concept: Show a connection between class concepts and personal experiences.
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can summarize short stories using simple drawings (e.g., draw a sunny scene and a swimmer). Can show a connection between class concepts and personal experiences by drawing or gesturing (e.g., drawing themselves running and saying, "I run fast."). Can connect familiar actions to new words (e.g., saying "I play." and drawing a ball).
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of key concepts for Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Move Like Me" TPR activity. The teacher says an action verb and performs it, e.g., "I run fast!" (teacher runs in place fast), "I swim slowly!" (teacher makes slow swimming motions). Students imitate the action and repeat the sentence. This activates vocabulary and pronunciation of /r/ and /s/. </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher shows a large picture of a child running in a park. The teacher draws a simple stick figure running on the board and says, "Look! A boy runs fast in the park. I draw a boy running. This means 'run fast'." The teacher then draws a sunny sky and says, "It is fun outdoors!" The teacher models summarizing a small scene with a simple drawing and connecting it to a sentence.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher runs fast) "Am I slow or fast?" (Students respond: "Fast!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher points to a drawing of a child playing soccer) "Is this 'run' or 'play soccer'?" (Students respond: "Play soccer!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Fun Outdoors!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large picture cards depicting a short story sequence: 1. A boy and girl playing soccer in a park. 2. The boy scores a goal. 3. They high-five and say "Fun!".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Action:</b> The teacher presents the picture cards one by one, describing each scene with simple sentences. "Look! A boy and a girl play soccer in the park. They love to play soccer!" (pronunciation of /s/ in "soccer"). "The boy kicks the ball! Goal! He is happy." "They are friends. Playing soccer is fun!" The teacher uses gestures to convey meaning.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Expressing preferences for activities: "I like to run. It's fun." Asking questions about others' preferences: "Do you like to play outside?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(After showing all cards) "What sport do they play?" (Students point to soccer ball / say "soccer").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are they happy or sad?" (Students show happy face / say "happy").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation (connecting concepts, summarizing with visuals, using simple sentences).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Grammar: Subject + verb + adverb ("I run fast."), Present simple ("I love to run."). Vocabulary: run, jump, swim, play, fast, slow, happy, fun, love.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Draw and Match:</b> The teacher provides students with a worksheet containing simple pictures of outdoor activities (e.g., a child running, a child swimming, a child jumping). Next to each picture, there are two sentences with a blank, e.g., "I ____ fast." or "I ____ slowly." Students draw a line from the picture to the correct sentence and then fill in the blank (e.g., "I <u>run</u> fast."). The teacher circulates, checking for understanding and correct matching.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>My Preference Drawing:</b> The teacher shows flashcards of different outdoor activities (run, swim, jump, play baseball). For each card, the teacher asks, "Do you like to [activity]?" Students raise a thumb up for "yes" and a thumb down for "no." Then, the teacher asks students to draw a simple picture of one activity they *love* to do. While they draw, the teacher circulates and asks, "What do you love to do?" prompting "I love to [activity]." (e.g., "I love to swim!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Show and Tell (Pair Mediation):</b> Students work in pairs. They show their drawing from the previous activity to their partner. They point to their drawing and say, "I love to [activity]. I [activity] [fast/slowly/high/low]." (e.g., "I love to run. I run fast!"). The partner listens and points to the drawing, repeating "You love to [activity]!" This practices summarizing their preference through a drawing and explaining it to a peer.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Fun Outdoors!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Students continue working on their "Outdoor Sports Journal" project. This stage focuses on creating a journal entry to summarize their favorite outdoor activity, connecting it to personal experience, and preparing to explain it. </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Action:</b> The teacher distributes blank journal pages or construction paper labeled "My Outdoor Sports Journal." Students choose *one* outdoor activity they love (e.g., run, swim, play soccer). They draw a clear picture of themselves doing this activity. Below the drawing, they write a simple sentence: "I love to [activity]. I [activity] [adverb/adjective]." (e.g., "I love to run. I run fast!"). The teacher reminds them to use bright colors and big drawings to summarize their experience clearly. The teacher prompts with questions like, "What do you love to do? Draw it big!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in expressing personal preference, using target vocabulary (verbs, adverbs), summarizing an experience visually and verbally, and preparing for a brief explanation. Emphasis on mediation by translating a personal experience into a simple visual and linguistic summary.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy for Mediation skills and project completion.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and peer review of journal entries.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their "Outdoor Sports Journal" page in small groups or to a partner. Each student shows their drawing and verbally explains their entry: "This is me. I love to [activity]. I [activity] [adverb]!" (e.g., "This is me. I love to swim. I swim slowly!"). Students then listen to their partner and give a "thumbs up" if they understood the drawing and sentence.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Summarizes a chosen outdoor activity using a clear drawing.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Connects class concepts (activities) to personal experiences through drawing and speech (e.g., draws self running, says "I run fast.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (e.g., love, run, swim, fast, slowly) and grammar (Subject + verb + adverb) intelligibly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation of /r/ and /s/ in key words is understandable.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer two questions by drawing a happy face or a sad face:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I draw what I love to do outdoors?" (Draw happy/sad face)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I tell my friend about my drawing?" (Draw happy/sad face)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What outdoor activity do you love the most?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it fun to draw and talk about it in English?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students were able to draw a clear representation of an outdoor activity and connect it to a simple sentence using "I love to..." and "I [verb] [adverb]." The peer presentation was effective for encouraging simple verbal mediation. Some students still need more practice with adverb placement and pronunciation of /r/. I need to continue reinforcing sentence structure and provide more explicit practice with adverbs in future lessons.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "Fun Outdoors!" theme. The next lesson will introduce a new theme and set of vocabulary, building upon the foundational communication skills developed in this sequence.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>